  Path reference artefacts (build123d/tests/test_underware_path_channels.py):
  the S connector is bezpath_curve's default 16 chords per Bezier segment, so
  its walls sit up to the chord sagitta off the port's true curve (0.27 mm
  computed at the defaults); each diagonal joint's outside fill is a
  rotate_extrude at $fa 12 (0.07 mm facet sagitta at r = 13.2).
  The default mitre's bend (r = h/2 = 11) is path_extrude2d segments with
  gaps between them: the reference holds ~1105 mm3 less than the port's
  revolve there, so it is matched off the bend only. The mitre is a library
  parity reference, NOT a registered model (mayor ruling on pst-fsk8s.3;
  print strategy pst-1nv9a).

Path channel deviations (build123d/holders/underware_path_channels.py header):
- S / diagonal cord cutouts cut the bottom run's walls; the source's cutouts
  cut only its grips.
- Named errors bound the S bend radius (the inner wall would fold under the
  half-width) and the diagonal mitre (the inside wall's setback past the end
  grip's 6.5 mm stand-back), and every combination that runs past the bed.

Frames: Z=0 is the closed, chamfered face; Y runs along the channel. The
source's 0.01 mm Nudge makes every mesh span y = -L/2-0.015 .. L/2-0.005;
the port spans exactly +/-L/2 (parity shifts the references +0.005 in Y).
CGAL keeps each grip as a separate volume touching the wall top ("Volumes:
12" in the I render), so the references show 1.30 mm down-facing grip-base
faces at z = height-3.4; the fused port has no such face.

Port deviations (build123d/holders/underware_i_channel.py header):
- Decorative / Support_Bridging_Separation (:88-92) not ported: the author's
  Decorative=true render is non-manifold (OpenSCAD warns) and its 0.4 mm
  feet are below the 0.9 mm wall floor.
- Label: single-material deboss only (Text_Emboss=true geometry), Inter Bold
  via build123d/labels/label_text.py instead of Raleway, text size scaled by
  1/0.72 to match OpenSCAD's em; no Text_x_coordinate, colours or inlay.
- Filament groove honours Filament_Diameter (the source hard-codes
  teardrop2d(d=1.75) at :331; identical at the 1.75 default).
- Channel_Length_Units max 11, not the spec's 12 (:44): 12 cells are 336 mm, past
  the 325 mm Bambu H2S single-nozzle bed (build123d/holders/printer.py).
- Suppress_Connectors / Channel_Separators: per-index codes as in the source
  (:89 N/L/R/B/S, :92 N/S); extra or unknown codes are named errors.

Junction deviations (build123d/holders/underware_junctions.py header):
- L / T / X: no label (the author's L:65 / T:67 / X:65 label is MakerWorld-3MF-
  only and Text_x_coordinate is one-dimensional on a multi-arm part); no
  Decorative. Arm lengths stop where one parameter alone still fits the bed
  (L arms 10, T stem 10, T arms 9, X arms 9); longer combinations and cutouts
  that run past the first channel are named errors.
- T cord cutouts cut both walls as Cord_Side_Cutouts names. The source's
  right_half (T:143-145) wraps the stem channel AND its cutout children, so
  its -X wall openings never cut ('Right Side' cuts nothing, 'Both Sides'
  equals 'Left Side', Top slots stop at x=0); L and X cut both walls with the
  same block. Parity masks exactly the clipped cutter volume (641 mm3 in
  t-s3-cutouts.stl).
- Corners are revolved, not mitred: path_extrude2d chains 89 straight
  segments (chord error 0.0005 mm at r=13.2).

Published exceptions (build123d/holders/underware_i_channel.py
audit_exclusions; tests/test_underware.py test_exception_probe_matches_reference):
- grip-end-wedge: grip-end 45 degree ramp (:233-252) meets the nub undercut;
  0.6 mm inside the outer face the wedge is 0.64 mm (both meshes).
- flare-slit-fin / floor: flare slits (:436-437) start at topChamfer=4 under
  the inset corner (4.83) and on the exterior chamfer tip: fin 0.41 mm at
  z=4.4, floor 0.6 mm at 0.6 mm in (flare5 reference).
- cutout-sill: side cutout floors (:170/:178) bottom out at z=4 where the
  exterior chamfer starts: 0.5 mm at 0.5 mm in (cutouts reference).
- label-recess: glyph recess ceilings at text depth (0.2, reference 0.205)
  are flat bridges no wider than a stroke; measured before exclusion.

Transition port deviations (build123d/holders/underware_transition.py header):
- Rise_Distance of the height change is whole 28 mm cells (the source's mm in
  12.5 mm steps, default 25); the width change and the bridge already use cells.
- Named-error domains where the author's geometry is unprintable: width-change
  jog wall under 0.9 mm, a transition as long as the channel (end wedge),
  a height change steeper than 45 degrees standing, cutouts / label / flare on
  the standing height change, bridge rises outside 28..55 mm (45 degree ramp,
  0.9 mm tilted floor; the bridge's to_height default is 28, not 22).

Transition published exceptions (build123d/holders/underware_transition.py
audit_exclusions; tests/test_underware_transition.py): the I channel's
grip-end-wedge, flare-slit-fin, cutout-sill and label-recess families for the
same author modules, plus height-change-step-patch: standing on end, the
downward 1.30 x 1.40 mm end of each grip rail, one per grip (rev 4 §8 as
pinned by the mayor's answer of 2026-10-14 04:4xZ), and bridge-end-wedge: each
one-cell bridge end ramps up from its end face, which meets the ramped floor
and inner chamfers in a 90 - atan(rise/28) degree wedge (45 at rise 28); the
envelope is the inner channel, SNAP_WALL deep from each end face, from the end
face's lower edge to its wall top (mayor-authorised, option A).

Bridge default deviation (parameter only; geometry untouched): the author's
default 22/22/22 (from/to/bridge height) rises 22 mm over one 28 mm cell, so
the reference bridge.stl's end-ramp undersides are 51.8 degrees from vertical
(1309.9 mm2, trimesh normals) -- over the 45 degree print limit base-down. The
port defaults to_height to 28 (on the step-6 grid), the smallest rise whose
ramps are printable (45.0 degrees, no bridge), and bounds the rise
max(bridge, from) + to - from to 28..55 with named errors (above 55 the 2 mm
floor tilts under 0.9 mm). mesh/bridge-to28.stl is the parity reference for the
port's default.

